    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Deleting an owner is now a soft-delete, and the full test suite passes (`./mvnw test`: 18 tests, 0 failures), including the two acceptance tests for this change.\n\n**What it does now**\n- `DELETE /api/owners/{id}` sets the owner's `deleted` flag to true and keeps the row. It still returns 204, or 404 if the owner doesn't exist.\n- `GET /api/owners/{id}` still returns a deleted owner, with `\"deleted\": true`. A newly created owner has `\"deleted\": false`.\n- When creating an owner, both blocking checks skip deleted owners: the household check (409 `DuplicateHousehold`) and the identity-key check (409 `DuplicateIdentity`). So re-creating an owner whose only match was deleted now succeeds.\n\n**Main changes**\n- `Owner.java`: new `deleted` field. All four database schemas (hsqldb, h2, mysql, postgres) get a `deleted BOOLEAN NOT NULL DEFAULT FALSE` column, so the existing seed data loads unchanged.\n- `ClinicServiceImpl.deleteOwner` now sets the flag and saves instead of deleting the record.\n- `OwnerRepository`: new `countNotDeletedByHouseholdId`, used for the household check. I implemented it in all three repository versions (Spring Data, JPA, JDBC). The JDBC version also reads and writes the new column.\n- `openapi.yml`: added `deleted` to the Owner schema and changed the DELETE description to say it's a soft-delete.\n\n**Decisions for you**\n- **Possible-duplicate flag:** I also made the non-blocking `possibleDuplicateOf` marker skip deleted owners, since it's a duplicate check too. Otherwise a new owner could be pointed at a deleted one. This is easy to undo if you'd rather it still counted them.\n- **Checks I left alone:** these still count deleted owners: city capacity, the daily owner limit, customer-code uniqueness, namesake count and household size. Customer-code uniqueness has to, because the rows still exist. The others could go either way; I left them because the spec only covered duplicate and identity checks.\n- **Owner lists:** listing and search endpoints still include deleted owners, since the spec didn't say to hide them.\n- **Other profiles:** I also ran the acceptance tests with the JDBC and JPA profiles selected and they passed. But I didn't confirm the profile override actually took effect, so treat those two as unconfirmed.",
